      "created_at": "2020-02-09T21:04:37Z",
      "updated_at": "2020-02-09T21:04:37Z",
      "body": "I think the ideal case is to define SQLFluff as a plugin host, similar to the recent work I did on `diff-quality`. Then other packages could register themselves as defining SQLFluff plugins. Then SQLFluff could easily \"discover\" those packages and import them. Given the way rules are defined with a decorator, e.g.:\r\n\r\n```\r\n@std_rule_set.register\r\nclass Rule_L001(BaseCrawler):\r\n```\r\n\r\nI think simply having SQLFluff import those libraries would be enough to incorporate them into the list of checks. There might be some additional considerations to make sure the class names or other identifying information doesn't \"collide\" with built-in checks. But overall, I think it wouldn't be that big of a change.\r\n\r\nI only thought of this idea recently when someone on our Security team was looking at some of our queries and asked, did you remember to use \"safe\" table X rather than unsafe table Y. This could be a common type of requirements at other companies, and being able to handle this would add a lot of value to the tool. General code quality is nice, but security and confidentiality are very important. ",
      "author_association": "MEMBER",
      "pin": null,
      "reactions": {
