      "created_at": "2020-11-30T17:39:22Z",
      "updated_at": "2020-12-03T15:04:14Z",
      "body": "Possible use cases for our organization:\r\n* On BigQuery, we prefer `USING` over `JOIN` for brevity and readability reasons, in spite of the general recommendation adopted for SQLFluff core, so we'd flag places where it could be used.\r\n* For privacy reasons, queries should avoid using certain of our internal tables, preferring related views that have been \"cleaned\" of users who have \"opted out\" from a GDPR/privacy perspective. If they do access these tables, they must `JOIN` with other tables to \"manually\" apply these filters.\r\n* (This one is still vague) If the result of a query reads from tables known to contain PII (personally identifiable information), it **must** retain the specific fields (e.g. `user_id`) that allow the output data to be tied back to the source. Maintaining this \"lineage\" / backtracking info is critical in order to allow this data to later be scanned, filtered, and purged of information for users who have said \"stop using my data for machine learning projects\". This is a legal requirement for ours and many other businesses.\r\n* If a query accesses an \"external\" table (i.e. from another GCP \"project\"), that table reference must be \"frozen\" to a particular date and time using the BigQuery `FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF` syntax. This policy assures that query results are reproducible even if the underlying table data changes\r\n  * backfills\r\n  * a job for day X runs on day X+1 due to lag\r\n  * a job fails and is retried\r\n\r\nThese are the ideas that are \"top of mind\". If it'd be helpful, I could take a closer look at some of our projects for other possible ideas. ",
      "author_association": "MEMBER",
      "pin": null,
